## The Problem

An independent artist has to be a lot of things at once, and a generic page makes all of them harder:

- **Many Audiences, One Page:** Listeners, venues, event organizers and collaborators all arrive wanting different things.
- **Scattered Presence:** Songs lived on Spotify, videos on YouTube and photos on Instagram, with no single place that told the whole story.
- **A Bilingual Story:** My music moves between Colombia and Canada, and the site needed to speak to people in both Spanish and English.

## The Goal

The main objective was a site that feels like me and works like a tool: something that makes it easy to listen, easy to follow and easy to book.

- **Lead With the Music:** Put listening and watching first, with the latest video and Spotify one click away.
- **Make Booking Obvious:** Show what I offer, upcoming shows and a clear way to get in touch.
- **Speak Both Languages:** Treat Spanish as a full version of the site, not an afterthought.
- **Keep It Editable:** Let me update shows, photos and copy myself without touching code.

## My Design & Development Process

### Brand & Look

The identity is built around warmth and memory. A dark, candlelit palette lets the photography glow, a flowing script wordmark gives the name a handwritten feel, and an arched photo frame echoes a window or a stage. Serif type carries the quotes and headings, with a clean sans for everything practical.

![The About section: serif headings, a peach accent, an arched photo frame and a circular artist badge on the dark palette](/images/projects/aniposada-about.webp)

### Structure

I organized the page around what each visitor needs, in order: listen, watch, learn who I am, browse the gallery, see upcoming shows, check services, support the music, and get in touch. Shows come with add-to-calendar links, and services are described in plain language so a restaurant owner or a couple planning a wedding knows exactly what they can ask for.

![The Music section with an embedded Spotify player and top tracks](/images/projects/aniposada-music-section.webp)

![The Gallery section with photo albums from live shows and festivals](/images/projects/aniposada-gallery.webp)

![The Upcoming shows list with dates, venues and add-to-calendar buttons](/images/projects/aniposada-shows.webp)

![The Services section describing live music, music production and songwriting](/images/projects/aniposada-services.webp)

### Technical Challenge: A Truly Bilingual Site

- **The Task:** English and Spanish needed to be equal citizens, with their own pages, not a translation bolted on.
- **The Result:** I built the site in Astro with a dedicated Spanish version, a language toggle in the header, and the language metadata search engines expect, so each audience lands on the right version.

![The Spanish version of the site next to the mobile layout](/images/projects/aniposada-bilingual.webp)

## Final Results & Reflection

This project let me bring my two worlds together: the designer and the musician. Building my own site meant every decision came from how I actually want people to experience my music.

- **Designing for Feeling:** The best choices were the quiet ones: the pace of the page, the photography, and a single serif voice for the words that matter.
- **One Page, Many Visitors:** Ordering the sections around what each audience needs made the site simple to scan without feeling like a form.
- **Owning the Stack:** Managing the content through a CMS means I can add a show or a photo in minutes, which keeps the site current.
